WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.100
RC, correct answers. What a fraught topic.

2
00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:06.520
Honestly, what has enacted more strife in LSAT?

3
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.140
Many things, but this is one of those things that's enacted a lot of strife.

4
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.620
Here, we're going to talk about learning to love the vanilla,

5
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:18.600
and that is the flavor of RC. I also like to say it's flavored like Soylent.

6
00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:22.580
If you ever remember, 10 plus years ago, when people were

7
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:26.520
drinking this food powder that tasted like pancake mix.

8
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.860
Once, I knew someone who drank a lot of Soylent. They were weird.

9
00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:30.600
But

10
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:35.380
Soylent. So why am I likening these RC correct answers to Soylent, to vanilla,

11
00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:39.420
to something really bland? It's because these answers that end up

12
00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:43.300
being correct, they often don't really feel like that kind of

13
00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:47.200
snap, crackle, pop of an LR correct answer.

14
00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:51.880
They feel kind of like a letdown of like, "I guess it's

15
00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:55.560
not wrong," and that's okay. You

16
00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:00.020
learn to love the vanilla. You learn to love that feeling of like,

17
00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.980
"Well, you're not wrong." And it's almost like this kind of jokey thing with a

18
00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:07.840
friend of, "You're not wrong." But the trouble comes when

19
00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:10.840
these not wrong, but Soylenty

20
00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:14.670
vanilla-tasting answers have another answer in the

21
00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.580
set that is a peak-and-valley answer.

22
00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.000
And there's a whole other video devoted to peaks and valleys that I will link to

23
00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:20.280
here,

24
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.340
but we'll give a brief overview so you can follow along.

25
00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.680
Peak-and-valley answer is one that has a lot of

26
00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.840
high-point fireworks in it. Your words directly from the passage,

27
00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:35.700
especially at the beginning of the answer, to lock you in and have you start

28
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.620
working for that answer as soon as you start reading it, and then it's got that

29
00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:43.440
deliciousness of a red juicy apple, and then just a

30
00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:47.280
little bit of poison. Just a little poison.

31
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.380
And the problem is, you still don't want to eat

32
00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:54.780
just a little bit of poison. It's still poison.

33
00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:59.660
And so even though you have those great parts with words

34
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:03.580
directly from the passage, you've also got that valley, that really low

35
00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:07.260
part that is definitely wrong, that

36
00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.700
is definitely not proven by the passage.

37
00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:13.340
And so the psychological problem that tends to happen for people is they

38
00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:17.020
see those high points and they're like, "Oh, man, that's a 10 out of 10.

39
00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:20.900
That's great. I love it. Oh, awesome." And then that

40
00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:24.880
red juicy apple is so delicious that it makes you want to eat the poison or forget

41
00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.460
the poison is there and be like, "Well, yeah, but this is the more important part

42
00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.100
of the answer." There is no more important part of the answer.

43
00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:33.480
The entire answer is the most important part of the answer.

44
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:37.420
And so you're in this situation, you have this red

45
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.120
juicy apple. I think that was in Snow White.

46
00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.420
Doesn't she like these sheets, and apple's bad? Probably.

47
00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.600
Versus the vanilla Soylent answer.

48
00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:48.820
And what tends to happen is the student will do some level of averaging

49
00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:53.000
of like, "Okay, well, this part of the peak-and-valley

50
00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.140
answer is a 10 out of 10, and then there's this other part that, I don't know,

51
00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:59.420
maybe it's a five," because they're doing favors for that part of the

52
00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:03.120
answer, trying to have it not be as terminally fatal.

53
00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.980
And they're like, "So it's a seven or something.

54
00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:09.800
And, oh, this other answer that's kind of vanilla and just

55
00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:13.480
like blah feeling, it's a six. And so actually, the

56
00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:14.980
peak-and-valley answer is, quote, 'better.'"

57
00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:17.620
No. The truth is that

58
00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:22.000
one part of an answer being wrong means the entire answer is

59
00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:22.600
wrong.

60
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:23.480
And

61
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:28.170
when you get that Soylenty kind of not very

62
00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:31.980
satisfying answer, let's talk about what those features of that answer

63
00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.800
are. It will very often have words that aren't in the passage.

64
00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:38.880
And this doesn't necessarily mean these words are unproven.

65
00:03:39.049 --> 00:03:42.450
It just means that you have to be thinking conceptually to understand how they're

66
00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.190
supported, instead of playing a sound-based

67
00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:49.880
matching game to the passage. The sound-based matching game of like, "It

68
00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:52.520
said feral. Feral."

69
00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:55.549
That's the peak-and-valley stuff.

70
00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:59.740
And not to say no correct answer ever has words from the passage, because of course

71
00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:02.940
they do. I'm just speaking to those more difficult answers that are sometimes hard

72
00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:06.850
to swallow. On this more vanilla Soylent answer, you're not

73
00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:10.240
going to see feral, feral, you're going to see feral and wild.

74
00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:14.860
And you're going to have to know that feral means

75
00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:17.829
wild. Let's say in the context of animal control.

76
00:04:19.221 --> 00:04:22.360
And so then you're going to have to do the

77
00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:26.380
understanding of the meaning of the answer to understand that this

78
00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:30.340
actually is supported by the passage, even if it doesn't feel as snap, crackle,

79
00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:34.280
pop. And this is okay. Very often, though,

80
00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:37.860
what I find is students will pathologize these vanilla

81
00:04:37.870 --> 00:04:41.670
answers as traps. They'll be like, "Oh, well, they

82
00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:45.390
said wild, and it was feral in the passage," or vice versa, whatever it

83
00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:47.240
is. And they're like, "That's a trap.

84
00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:49.000
They're trying to get me." And so

85
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.900
this will often lead to weird non-proving

86
00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:57.920
based actions in the student. So suddenly they're stopping just asking, "Okay,

87
00:04:58.040 --> 00:04:59.980
well, does the passage support the truth of this answer?

88
00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:04.000
Does this make sense?" Et cetera. Whatever the question is asking, you should

89
00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:06.850
have it informed by the actual text of the question when you're doing this

90
00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:10.200
select-an-answer. They'll stop being informed by the actual text of the question,

91
00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:13.540
and they'll start asking, "Is this a trap? Is this a trick?

92
00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:16.210
Oh, am I going to fall for something?

93
00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:20.240
Oh, this is them victimizing me." Get rid of all of that interior

94
00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.730
monologue, because what it does is it distracts you from the actual metrics that

95
00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:27.680
make an answer right. And, of course, we'll talk later

96
00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:31.410
about using the actual question stem to your advantage and letting that

97
00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:35.160
be how you select answers, but you just want to ask yourself,

98
00:05:35.539 --> 00:05:36.120
"Is it

99
00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:39.360
wrong?" No.

100
00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:44.170
Even if it isn't what you came up with in your mission, and we'll

101
00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:45.270
talk more about missioning later,

102
00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:49.960
is it wrong, wrong, or does it just not feel comfy?

103
00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:52.380
It's kind of like, does the shirt technically fit?

104
00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.720
And it is a shirt that covers me and allows me to go outside?

105
00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:56.520
Or

106
00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:59.000
is it not?

107
00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:03.420
Even if it isn't the best fit of any shirt I've ever worn,

108
00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:07.760
it's a serviceable fit. That's the feeling

109
00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:11.510
of a correct answer. And so I know that you cannot

110
00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:15.400
afford to have an aversion to just pathologizing any answer that

111
00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:16.680
has words that aren't in the passage.

112
00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:20.670
It will make you start doing weird selective blindness maneuvers to make excuses

113
00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:24.539
for the valleys, parts of peaks and valleys. And so I don't want that for you.

114
00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:28.100
I want you to just deeply investigate the true nature of the understanding of each

115
00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:31.980
answer choice, apply what the question stem is asking, and accept

116
00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:35.780
that sometimes that kind of mediocre vanilla, it's

117
00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:39.640
not wrong answer is right. And by sometimes, I mean all the

118
00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:41.980
time. That's what a right answer feels like in RC.

119
00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:45.300
And so we'll get back to this soon, and I will talk to you later

